Every entity, regardless of its nature, scale, capability, authority, DOS, or degree of dynamic coupling with other entities, is fundamentally bounded in its ability to completely specify, observe, comprehend, or determine the totality of the reality in which it exists and participates.

An entity’s Dynamic Operational Specification (DOS) defines the operational structure through which that entity participates in reality: its role, purpose, capabilities, authority, permissions, constraints, resources, information requirements, decision boundaries, objectives, dependencies, and escalation conditions. Dynamic coupling defines how those entities influence and are influenced by one another across changing configurations and time.

However, neither DOS nor dynamic coupling eliminates fundamental incompleteness.

Every entity therefore operates from a necessarily incomplete representation of reality. There will always exist states, relationships, consequences, configurations, or information that lie beyond the entity’s complete determination at a given point in its existence.

This incompleteness shall not automatically be regarded as a defect.

The unknown may itself be a constitutive condition of continuity. The inability of an entity to completely determine reality may permit emergence, interaction, adaptation, transformation, creation, dissolution, and succession of entities. What an entity cannot know may therefore participate in determining what it can become.

Accordingly, Safe & Sane shall not treat complete knowledge, complete prediction, or complete control as achievable prerequisites for legitimate governance.

Instead, governance shall seek to:

preserve the capacity of entities to safely continue, adapt, interact, transform, and recover under conditions of fundamental uncertainty.

The possibility that an entity’s incompleteness may contribute to its finite lifetime, transformation, dissolution, or replacement remains an open hypothesis, not an established law.

Therefore:

No entity shall be granted absolute epistemic authority over the reality in which it participates merely because of its capabilities, knowledge, DOS, computational power, or authority.

And Safe & Sane itself remains subject to the same fundamental limitation.

The governance system must therefore govern not from presumed completeness, but from acknowledged incompleteness.
